A list holding one item is read twice before anything is added to it:
`const ul = document.getElementById("l");
const snap = ul.querySelectorAll("li");
const live = ul.getElementsByTagName("li");
ul.appendChild(document.createElement("li"));
console.log(snap.length + " and " + live.length);`
Given the initial markup `<ul id="l"><li>a</li></ul>`, the line prints 1 and 2. Which of the two collections is tied to the tree, and what makes the other one a photograph rather than a window?

A span already sits inside the first of two empty-capable elements:
`<div id="p1"><span id="s">x</span></div><div id="p2"></div>`
`const p1 = document.getElementById("p1"), p2 = document.getElementById("p2");
const s = document.getElementById("s");
const r = p2.appendChild(s);
console.log(r.id, p1.children.length, p2.children.length, s.parentElement.id);`
The line prints s, 0, 1, and p2. Explain what the call did to the span's membership of the first element, and say why the four printed values cannot all be right if it copied instead.

A list holds two items and a third element is made and given the identifier c:
`<ul id="l"><li id="a">a</li><li id="b">b</li></ul>`
`const ul = document.getElementById("l");
const c = document.createElement("li"); c.id = "c";
const r = ul.insertBefore(c, document.getElementById("b"));
console.log(r.id + " and " + [...ul.children].map(e => e.id).join(", "));`
The line prints c and a, c, b. State the two decisions the call makes, and say which of them the return value records.

The second item of a list carries a span inside it, and the item is removed by hand rather than by emptying the list:
`<ul id="l"><li id="a">a</li><li id="b"><span id="in">x</span></li></ul>`
`const ul = document.getElementById("l"), b = document.getElementById("b");
const r = ul.removeChild(b);
console.log(r.id, r.parentNode, ul.children.length, r.childNodes.length);`
Printing the parent by its node name, the line prints b, null, 1, and 1. Say what the fourth value proves about the subtree, and why the second value is not the list.

A list holds two items and a replacement item with the identifier c is made:
`<ul id="l"><li id="a">a</li><li id="b">b</li></ul>`
`const ul = document.getElementById("l"), b = document.getElementById("b");
const c = document.createElement("li"); c.id = "c";
const r = ul.replaceChild(c, b);
console.log(r.id, r.parentNode === null ? "null" : r.parentNode.id, [...ul.children].map(e => e.id).join(", "), b.childNodes.length);`
The line prints b, null, a, c, and 1. Name the node that comes back and the node that leaves, and say what the second value says about the one that left.

A list holds one item and a fragment is given two more items before the list is asked to take the whole batch:
`<ul id="l"><li id="k">k</li></ul>`
`const ul = document.getElementById("l");
const f = document.createDocumentFragment();
for (const id of ["m","n"]) { const li = document.createElement("li"); li.id = id; f.appendChild(li); }
const before = f.childNodes.length;
ul.appendChild(f);
console.log(before, f.childNodes.length, [...ul.children].map(e => e.id).join(", "));`
The line prints 2, 0, and k, m, n. Say what the second value proves about the second use of the same fragment, and where the new items sit relative to the old one.

A copy is taken of an element that holds a bold run and a plain run of text:
`<div id="s"><b>bold</b>tail</div>`
`const s = document.getElementById("s");
const deep = s.cloneNode(true), shallow = s.cloneNode();
console.log(deep.textContent.length, deep.children.length, deep.childNodes.length, shallow.childNodes.length);`
The line prints 8, 1, 2, and 0. What does the argument decide about the copy, and why is the deep copy's child count one higher than its element child count?

An element holds text on both sides of a nested pair of elements:
`<div id="d">a<span>b<i>c</i></span>d</div>`
`const d = document.getElementById("d");
const before = d.textContent;
d.textContent = "z";
console.log(before, d.children.length, d.childNodes.length, d.textContent.length);`
The line prints abcd, 0, 1, and 1. Say what the first value proves about reading, and what the second and third prove about the assignment.

An input is given three attributes in markup and a fourth is written by the program with a mixed-case name:
`<input id="i" class="a b" value="v">`
`const i = document.getElementById("i");
i.setAttribute("data-Foo", "X");
console.log(i.getAttributeNames().pop(), i.attributes.length, i.getAttribute("CLASS"), i.classList.length);`
The line prints data-foo, 4, a b, and 2. Say what happens to the case of the written name, and why the lookup by the upper-case class name still succeeds.

A form holds one field whose markup carries an initial value, and a program overwrites the field and then resets the form:
`<form id="f"><input id="i" name="q" value="start"></form>`
`const f = document.getElementById("f"), i = document.getElementById("i");
i.value = "typed";
const after = i.value + " and " + i.getAttribute("value");
f.reset();
console.log(after, "and", i.value);`
The line prints typed and start, and start. A form restores itself by restoring the values the markup carried, not the values a user or a program put in. Say which of the two values each half of the program is reporting, and why the third reading is not a bug.

A div sits in the body and a run of text is placed in a holder that is not part of the document:
`<body>\n<div id="d"></div>\n</body>`
`const div = document.getElementById("d");
const holder = document.createDocumentFragment();
const loose = document.createTextNode("x"); holder.appendChild(loose);
console.log(div.parentElement.nodeName, div.parentNode.nodeName, loose.parentElement === null ? "null" : "not null", loose.parentNode.nodeName);`
The line prints BODY, BODY, null, and #document-fragment. Say which of the two parent properties is the one to reach for when a loop is going to call element methods, and what the third value means.

A program sets one data attribute twice and then adds a class to an element that has an identifier:
`<div id="d"></div>`
`const el = document.getElementById("d");
el.setAttribute("data-x", "1");
el.setAttribute("data-x", "2");
el.setAttribute("class", "c");
console.log(el.attributes.length, el.getAttributeNames().filter(n => n === "data-x").length, el.getAttribute("data-x"));`
The line prints 3, 1, and 2. A reviewer claims the second write added a second attribute and that the first value is the one that wins. Explain why the element's attributes are a set of names rather than a record of writes, and use the three numbers to show what that model predicts.
